@ 10.5 - Proto-Austronesian to Proto-Ongan
# citation: Pogostick Man, from Blevins, Juliette (2007), “A Long Lost Sister of Proto-Austronesian? Proto-Ongan, Mother of Jarawa and Onge of the Andaman Islands”. Oceanic Linguistics 46(I):154 – 198 NB: Blevins floats the idea that Proto-Ongan was a sister of rather than a daughter of Proto-Austronesian, but for reasons of simplicity in editing this document it is placed here.
	b > ∅ / :{ #_u, #_i }:
	q > ∅ / #_V
	q > k
	{qʷ,ku,qu} > kʷ (note that P:[+affricate]n might have had *qʷ *kʷ
	kʷ (note that P:[+affricate]n might have had *qʷ *kʷ > q {k,w} instead; may be a change from POn-P:[+affricate]n, if it existed)
	{c,*C,s,*S} > c (again, possibly a change from POn-P:[+affricate]n, if it existed)
	S > ∅ / _#
	{ u, a, ə } > { {u,o}, {a,e}, e }
	{ ɟ, ɡ, *N, *S } > { {ɟ,j}, {ɟ,ɡ}, {l,j}, {l,r} }
	z > c (again, possibly evidence of a change from POn-P:[+affricate]n, if it existed)
	h > {h,j,∅} ([+back]levins has marked what apparently is *ɟ but I’m assuming it’s an error)
	e > ə / _N when unstressed , but not listed on the correspondence list) // Ḱ_ (?; included here based on a comment earlier in the paper
	{ {m,ɲ}, n } > { {ɲ,∅}, {ŋ,∅} } / _# (first change marked in progress?)
	aj > e